#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b885b18-3e83-562e-aa15-bede595287dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4=2t,\\,\\,y-4={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310393} {:answer/id #uuid "65732b42-6809-5a6d-8d75-341d87c88a0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310394} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf074c0-d547-57da-90fc-af45742a3986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4=2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310395} {:answer/id #uuid "5d878cd8-ea9d-571e-b2c4-2747cdeb4e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4=2t,\\,\\,y+4={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310396} {:answer/id #uuid "5aebc749-f346-5867-826e-d05adad1e8d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y+4=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65732b42-6809-5a6d-8d75-341d87c88a0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310394} :answer-field/id #uuid "1938660b-7d05-579b-9d0b-397ac95ff0de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14e2d3b4-1c1a-54b9-9aa2-2d7f1398ec81" :question/math-academy-id "q-106461" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/e5c7f025b6f12599a4ecb5fa0cc9e657fd0b67deb1e50231673113f337ca7776.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(4,4),$ and passes through the point $(8,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(4,4),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-4=p{t}^{2},\\,y-4=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(8,8),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=8$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-4=(1){t}^{2},\\,y-4=2(1)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-4={t}^{2},\\,y-4=2t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5cfbb25-241e-55fa-a7d4-89ae164d5aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310421} {:answer/id #uuid "668b71eb-4d27-5add-b663-e98d9703dc8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310422} {:answer/id #uuid "6daa4abe-4ca9-5198-82b7-01d76b078024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310423} {:answer/id #uuid "9e016e23-f067-5f42-adf8-de94c24af360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-2{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310424} {:answer/id #uuid "a0dc9526-9759-5a8d-99ba-f52ca3e1c07f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5cfbb25-241e-55fa-a7d4-89ae164d5aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310421} :answer-field/id #uuid "88f24eaa-bd31-5c6c-80a0-5608034660ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81c9b11f-76b1-5474-bb7c-bc45490a8350" :question/math-academy-id "q-84842" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/7cb71517fe60c9d15b6cacddbf5f91c2e4a60103b8917a4d9baa287517a5fd54.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,-1),$ and passes through the point $(-5,1).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-3,-1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,1),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=1$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3=(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(-\\frac{1}{2})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t.\n$$"}]}
